So **the route that applies to attention is `/home/lihuzhan/code/2026_0910__op-evolve/op-evolve/knowledge/optimization/routes/1-metrics-to-techniques.md`** — and `0-kernel-programming-principles.md` "applies to every one of them without qualification," read before a kernel exists and again at plan time.

The README's rationale for one file covering all three op types: "the **metrics and their normalisation are identical** — a spill, an under-filled grid and a bank conflict do not care what the algorithm is, and `SQ_VALU_MFMA_BUSY_CYCLES / (SIMDs_per_XCD x GRBM_COUNT)` is the same ratio in all of them." (Note: that normalisation is gfx942/gfx950-only — `SQ_VALU_MFMA_BUSY_CYCLES` does not exist on gfx1250.)

**`1-metrics-to-techniques.md`** (203 lines, `status: from documentation`, scope `gfx942/gfx950, gemm/grouped_gemm/attention`):

- `## How to read the two tables`
- `## Table A -- metrics that invalidate other metrics` — evaluate top to bottom, **stop at the first one that fires**; that row is the work, re-take the profile afterwards. Spill is the strongest case: scratch traffic pollutes every memory counter, so a memory-bound diagnosis taken with non-zero spill is describing the spill.
- `## Table B -- diagnostic metrics` — each row indexed by a derived metric with its formula and the condition under which it is readable at all, pointing at one file in `optimization/techniques/`.
