## 6. `knowledge/arch/gfx1250/` — three files

| File | Lines | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gfx1250.md` | 196 | `reference` — "Nothing here is measured." Transcribed from AMD "CDNA5" ISA Reference Guide, 27-July-2026 |
| `isa.md` | 237 | `reference` — same source document |
| `profiling-surface.md` | 215 | **`verified`** — MI455X engineering sample (Card SKU M4500001, chip 0x75c1), ROCm 7.14.60850, PyTorch 2.11.0+rocm7.14, host `heliosr-1b114-c07-1`, 2026-08-31 |

There is **no** `performance-counters.md` and **no** `performance-model.md` for gfx1250 (both exist for gfx942 and gfx950). `profiling-surface.md` says why: "This page is deliberately **not** named `performance-counters.md`. There is no counter reference to write yet."

### Profiling surface / counters

This is the single most consequential file for your job.

- **51 counters defined for gfx1250** in `$ROCM_PATH/share/rocprofiler-sdk/config.yaml` — the smallest of any architecture in the ROCm database, smaller than Vega. gfx950 has 630, gfx942 420.
- **Only 13 return non-zero data.** The other 38 are defined, accepted by `rocprofv3`, and read zero. The working 13: `GRBM_COUNT`, `GRBM_CPC_BUSY`, `GRBM_GUI_ACTIVE`, `GRBMA_COUNT`, `GRBMH_COUNT`, `GRBMH_SPI_BUSY`, `SQC_ICACHE_REQ`, `SQC_ICACHE_MISSES`, `SQC_ICACHE_MISSES_DUPLICATE`, `SQ_WAVES`, `SQ_CYCLES`, `SQ_BUSY_CYCLES`, `SQ_ITEMS`. "That is: wall-clock cycles, wave count, and instruction-cache behaviour. Nothing about memory, nothing about the matrix unit, nothing about occupancy, nothing about stalls."
- **Entire blocks gone:** TCC (L2) 133→0, TCP (vL1D) 124→0, TA+TD 60→0, derived metrics 47→0, SQ 116→16, SPI 81→1. New RDNA-vocabulary blocks (GL1A, GL1C, GLARBC, CHA, CHC, GRBMA, GRBMH) exist but are "nearly empty". **"gfx1250 exposes no counter that reports bytes, at any level of the hierarchy."**
- **The WMMA FLOP counters read zero.** A kernel executing exactly 4,096,000 `v_wmma_f32_16x16x32_bf16` instructions reports `SQ_INSTS_VEC32_VALU_WMMA`, `SQ_INST_CYCLES_VALU_WMMA` and `SQ_VALU_WMMA_FLOP_BF16` all exactly 0, while `SQ_WAVES` in the same run is exact. The whole `SQ_VALU_WMMA_FLOP_*` family is marked `{emulated}` in its own description text and none of it works. A real hipBLASLt bf16 GEMM behaves the same way.
- **Silent failure trap:** `rocprofv3 --pmc TCC_HIT_sum SQ_INSTS_VALU ...` exits **0** and writes no CSV, no warning. "A profiling script ported from gfx950 therefore 'succeeds' and produces nothing. Any harness that runs `rocprofv3` on gfx1250 must assert that the output CSV exists and contains the counters it asked for; exit status is not a signal."
- **What does work:** stochastic PC sampling (the one rich source) and ATT. Host-trap PC sampling and SPM are advertised but produce no output. PC sampling reports per sample: instruction text, `Instruction_Type`, `Stall_Reason`, `Wave_Count`, `Exec_Mask`, whether the wave issued. Measured separation: WMMA kernel = 76.5% `ARBITER_NOT_WIN`, 81.9% `MATRIX`, 82.8% issue rate, mean Wave_Count 1.4; 2 GiB streaming kernel = 49.7% `WAITCNT` + 41.1% `OTHER_WAIT`, 97.4% `NO_INST`, 2.2% issue, mean Wave_Count 7.8. Invocation:
  ```
  rocprofv3 --pc-sampling-beta-enabled --pc-sampling-method stochastic \
    --pc-sampling-unit cycles --pc-sampling-interval 2048 \
    --output-format csv --output-file pc -d out -- ./app
  ```
  Valid interval 256–1048576 cycles.
- **What it falsifies elsewhere:** `profiling/3-bound-analysis.md`'s byte accounting (`TCC_*_SECTORS x 32`, `TCC_EA0_*_DRAM_32B x 32`) and its `SQ_LDS_IDX_ACTIVE` LDS-bandwidth row — gone. `profiling/2-power-wall-analysis.md`'s `SQ_WAIT_ANY` / `SQ_ACTIVE_INST_ANY` / `SQ_BUSY_CU_CYCLES` / `SQ_VALU_MFMA_BUSY_CYCLES` — none of the four exists.
- **Open question, explicitly not to be recorded as fact:** whether the 38 dead counters are unwired silicon or unimplemented ROCm 7.14.
- **Machine shape observed** (incidental): 256 CUs, 4 SIMDs/CU, wave32, 16 shader engines, 2 shader arrays/engine, 320 KB LDS, 432 GiB HBM; counter dimensions report 8 XCCs and 2 AIDs. One unresolved inconsistency: counter dims report `WGP[0:8]` (9 WGPs/array) against an arithmetic 4.

### wave32 vs wave64

- Wavefront size **32** (ISA §1). "**Wave64 is gone.** The ISA document opens by saying it may refer to wave64 but the device supports only wave32."
- "Every lane-count assumption in a kernel is wrong by a factor of two: cross-lane reductions, `EXEC` mask arithmetic, the lane-to-matrix-element mappings in any hand-written matrix layout, and the occupancy arithmetic that turns a register budget into waves."
- `EXEC` and `VCC` remain 64-bit registers; only bits [31:0] have effect; `EXECZ`/`VCCZ` test that half.
- `DS_PERMUTE_B32` / `DS_BPERMUTE_B32` now swizzle across 32 lanes — only index bits [6:2] used.

### WMMA (and the absence of MFMA)

- "**MFMA does not exist on this part.** `V_MFMA` appears 163 times in the CDNA 3 guide, 150 times in the CDNA 4 guide, and **zero** times in the CDNA 5 guide. Neither does `V_SMFMAC`. This is not a rename."
- Matrix work is `V_WMMA` (dense) and `V_SWMMAC` (2:4 sparse), VOP3P-encoded, `A * B + C => D`, one matrix striped across the wave (§7.12).
- **For bf16 the relevant shape is `V_WMMA_F32_16X16X32_BF16`** (A 16x32 BF16, B 32x16 BF16, C/D 16x16 F32). Also `V_WMMA_BF16_16X16X32_BF16` and `V_WMMA_BF16F32_16X16X32_BF16` (C = F32, D = BF16 — useful for a bf16 output). Note **M is 16 for every shape except `V_WMMA_SCALE*_F32_32X16X128_F4`**. There is no 32x32 tile as on CDNA3/4.
- Hard restrictions on every WMMA/SWMMAC: **wave32 only**; **`EXEC` must be all ones** ("Not honoured-and-ignored — required"); **DPP not supported**; round-to-nearest-even only; `MODE.Denorm` ignored and denorms **preserved, never flushed**; any NaN input → NaN, `0*Inf` → NaN; no exceptions.
- Operand rules: `SRC0`/`SRC1`/`VDST` must be **even-aligned VGPRs**; `SRC2` an even VGPR or inline zero. `OPSEL[2]`/`OPSEL_HI[2]` are **A-reuse / B-reuse hints** — "set them only when the current instruction is identical to the previous one, otherwise the result is undefined."
- **Hazards: there is no interlock.** Reading a previous matrix result as the A/B/index operand of another matrix instruction after `WMMA_*F16`/`WMMA_*BF16` needs **5 independent instructions (1 NOP + 4 co-exec slots)**, or 1 `V_NOP` with co-exec disabled. FP8/BF8 at 16x16x64 needs 2; at 16x16x128 needs 4; `WMMA_IU8` needs 9; `SWMMAC_*{F16,BF16,FP8,BF8}` needs 3. A following **VALU** that reads D (RAW), writes D (WAW) or writes A/B/index (WAR) needs a different count — 4 co-exec slots after `WMMA_*F16`/`*BF16` — and no `V_NOP` at all with co-exec disabled. "The slots can be filled by co-executing work, the leading NOP cannot."
- **Transposing loads** (§11.2.4), all wave32-only and `EXEC` ignored/all-ones: `DS_LOAD_TR16_B128` / `GLOBAL_LOAD_TR16_B128` (16-bit), `DS_LOAD_TR8_B64`, `DS_LOAD_TR6_B96`, `DS_LOAD_TR4_B64`. The 16-bit one is the gfx1250 analogue of the `ds_read_b64_tr_b16` the AITER attention card documents on gfx950 — relevant for the backward's K^T/V^T tiles.

### LDS

| | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| LDS per workgroup | **up to 320 KB** (0–320 KB) | ISA §2.2, §3.3.4 |
| Allocation granularity | 2048 bytes | ISA §3.3.4 |
| Physical memory | 320 KB LDS + 64 KB WGP$, **one structure with a configurable split** | ISA §1.2.2.1, §11.1 |
| Banks | **64, 4 bytes wide** | ISA §11.1 |

- vs gfx950: 160 KB → 320 KB per workgroup. Bank count unchanged (64).
- "**LDS bank count does not change from gfx950.** 64 banks of 4 bytes on both. A swizzle derived for gfx950 has the right modulus — but it was derived for 64 lanes issuing in phases, and the wave is now 32 lanes, so the phase structure it was tuned against is different. **Re-derive rather than assume**" (method in `optimization/techniques/2-lds-bank-conflicts.md`).
- Accesses bounds-checked against LDS base/size registers the shader cannot write. Every SIMD in the WGP can reach both LDS and WGP$.
- Waves are now grouped under a **WGP**, not a CU. 4 SIMDs (SIMD32) per WGP, 32 workgroups per WGP, max 1024 work-items per workgroup.

### Async data movement: TDM / tensor descriptors, clusters, MWAIT

**Tensor Data Mover (TDM)** — ISA §1.2.2.1, §10.11. One per pair of SIMDs. Instructions: `TENSOR_LOAD_TO_LDS`, `TENSOR_STORE_FROM_LDS`.

- "**It uses no VGPRs and is not issued per lane.** The tensor and tile are described by a descriptor (`D#`) held in SGPRs — 2 SGPR groups for tensors up to 2D, 4 groups for up to 5D."
- "**`EXEC` is ignored entirely.** The instruction issues whether `EXEC` is zero or not."
- "**It has its own completion counter,** `TENSORcnt`, waited on with `S_WAIT_TENSORCNT`. Tensor instructions stay in order with each other but are unordered with respect to every other kind of memory instruction. Completion is reported once per instruction, not per memory transfer."
- "**Tile data can be padded in LDS on a regular basis** to help a transpose or to avoid bank conflicts — the padding is part of the descriptor rather than something the addressing math has to carry."
- Tensor instructions may not appear in a clause.
- In a cluster, `D#.workgroup_mask` lets one TDM instruction land data in the LDS of several WGPs at once; must be zero outside a cluster.

**Workgroup clusters** — ISA §2.3. Up to 16 workgroups, 1D/2D/3D, scheduled onto the same shader engine; **each workgroup in a cluster runs on a separate WGP**. Grid hierarchy gains a level: Grid → Cluster → Workgroup → Wave → Work-item. Cluster-wide split barrier (signal + arrive) plus a second reserved for trap sync. Multicast loads: `CLUSTER_LOAD_B{32,64,128}` (tracked by `LOADcnt`) and `CLUSTER_LOAD_ASYNC_TO_LDS_B{32,64,128}` (tracked by `ASYNCcnt`); both **force a miss in the WGP$** regardless of scope/temporal bits. `Cluster_ID == 0` means not in a cluster and every cluster-barrier instruction becomes a NOP. Position delivered in `TTMP6`/`TTMP7`/`TTMP9`.

**MWAIT** — ISA §10.10. `GLOBAL_LOAD_MONITOR_{B32,B64,B128}` (or FLAT) arms an L2 cacheline watch and returns data immediately; `S_MONITOR_SLEEP` parks the wave. "**Wake-ups are not precise.**" Any watched line modified or evicted wakes *every* waiting wave. The "sleep forever" encoding is documented as unsafe.

**Six wait counters replace `vmcnt`/`lgkmcnt`** (§5.7.1) — they count **instructions, not threads**:

| Counter | Bits | Counts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `LOADcnt` | 6 | vector loads and atomics-with-return, `global_inv` |
| `STOREcnt` | 6 | vector stores and atomics-without-return, `global_wb`/`wbinv` |
| `DScnt` | 6 | LDS instructions |
| `KMcnt` | 5 | scalar loads and `S_SENDMSG` |
| `ASYNCcnt` | 6 | async loads/stores incl. `CLUSTER_LOAD_ASYNC_TO_LDS_*` |
| `TENSORcnt` | 6 | TDM transfers |

Waits: `S_WAIT_LOADCNT`, `S_WAIT_STORECNT`, `S_WAIT_DSCNT`, `S_WAIT_KMCNT`, `S_WAIT_ASYNCCNT`, `S_WAIT_TENSORCNT`, plus fused `S_WAIT_LOADCNT_DSCNT` / `S_WAIT_STORECNT_DSCNT` and `S_WAIT_XCNT`. **"Loads and stores no longer share a counter"** — this directly retires the constraint behind `backends/flydsl/attention/techniques.md`'s "One `vmcnt`, shared by loads and stores" section: "a drain carried over from gfx950 is leaving something on the table as well as being mis-spelled." `S_WAIT_KMcnt 0` is the only legitimate value with SMEM loads outstanding (scalar loads return out of order).

**Cache scope** replaces `SC0`/`SC1`/`NT` with a 2-bit `SCOPE` (0 `WGP`, 1 `SE`, 2 `DEV`, 3 `SYS`) plus a temporal hint (§4.1.1). Explicit prefetch: `FLAT_PREFETCH_B8`, `GLOBAL_PREFETCH_B8`, taking a `SCOPE` that selects the cache level.

### Occupancy and VGPR limits

- **VGPRs addressable: V0–V1023** (up from V0–V255 on gfx942/gfx950) — one flat file.
- **Accumulation VGPRs: none.** "`AccVGPR`, `ACCVGPR`, `AGPR` and `AV0-AV255` all appear zero times in the CDNA 5 guide… Anything in this corpus about splitting a register budget between arch and accumulation registers — `optimization/techniques/0-register-pressure-and-occupancy.md` in particular, whose '256 VGPR + 256 AGPR at 1 wave/SIMD' is a CDNA 3/4 fact — **does not describe this part**."
- SGPRs: S0–S105 (106 per wave) plus 16 TTMP. PC is 57 bits.
- **"No waves-per-SIMD limit"** is stated explicitly as a gap: "that last one is worth naming because third-party analyses of the LLVM backend quote a figure for it and the ISA document does not contain one."
- Also absent from the ISA doc and therefore from this branch: CU/XCD count, clock, HBM capacity, bandwidth, per-datatype peak throughput, PMC counter list. "Fill these from hardware when a part is available; until then, this file supports porting decisions and **not roofline arithmetic**."
- Practical consequence: there is no occupancy counter on gfx1250 either (`OccupancyPercent`, `MeanOccupancyPerCU` have no definition), so occupancy has to be read from the compiler resource report or inferred from PC sampling's `Wave_Count`.
- **Shader tail padding is a hard requirement:** 64 DWORDs (256 bytes) past the last instruction (ISA §2.4), `S_CODE_END` recommended as filler. "CDNA 5 prefetches instructions aggressively enough to run off the end of a kernel."

